ive.\\n\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\n\\nHard rules:\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\"Row 1\"), generic headers (\"Item\"/\"Detail\"),\\n  or \"Content pending.\"\\n\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\n- paragraph \u2192 { \"text\": string }\\n- heading \u2192 { \"text\": string, \"level\": 1|2|3 }\\n- list \u2192 { \"ordered\": boolean, \"items\": [string, ...] }\\n- table \u2192 { \"headers\": [string, ...], \"rows\": [[string, ...], ...], \"caption\": string }\\n- callout \u2192 { \"tone\": \"note\"|\"warning\"|\"tip\"|\"important\", \"title\": string, \"body\": string }\\n- figure \u2192 { \"caption\": string, \"alt\": string }\\n\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\n\\n\\n## AUTHORING DEFINITION\\n{\\n  \"assessment\": {},\\n  \"capability_id\": \"document-writer:paragraph\",\\n  \"converter_ref\": null,\\n  \"definition_hash\": \"c24bc83c1b6259ec1b3c6c18b2e06a94499913121902ea0dcab3b68c3ffafde1:paragraph\",\\n  \"instructions\": \"You write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\\\n\\\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\\\n\\\\nHard rules:\\\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"kind\": {\\n        \"const\": \"paragraph\"\\n      },\\n      \"text\": {\\n        \"minLength\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"text\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"kind\",\\n    \"brief\",\\n    \"teaching_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Build the idea on the same windowsill plant: the roots draw soil materials and water into the plant, but what enters through the roots is raw material the plant uses, not the plant\\'s food. The food itself is made by the plant. Hold the learner\\'s attention on the plant as the maker and treat soil and water only as intakes, so the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings are undermined without being named as errors yet. Do not introduce the sunlight-as-food idea and do not treat food and energy as interchangeable.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Introduces the category term \\'producer\\' to be fixed, keeping attention on the definition rather than the mechanism.\",\\n        \"role\": \"term-heading\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"States the single idea that a producer is a living thing that makes its own food and that the windowsill plant is a producer, with no sunlight or mechanism yet.\",\\n        \"role\": \"definition\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"list\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Lists the two intakes and the made food compactly so the intake-versus-made boundary stays visible on the same plant.\",\\n        \"role\": \"side-by-side\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"figure\",\\n        \"reason\": \"An illustrative figure of the windowsill plant showing roots taking in soil materials and water while food is made inside the plant reinforces the intake-versus-made distinction; asset generation uses the existing figure pipeline.\",\\n        \"role\": \"plant-anatomy-cue\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"Separates what roots draw in (soil materials and water as raw materials) from the food the plant makes itself, relocating food-making inside the plant without yet naming errors.\",\\n    \"role\": \"intake-vs-made\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Build the idea on the same windowsill plant: the roots draw soil materials and water into the plant, but what enters through the roots is raw material the plant uses, not the plant\\'s food. The food itself is made by the plant. Hold the learner\\'s attention on the plant as the maker and treat soil and water only as intakes, so the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings are undermined without being named as errors yet. Do not introduce the sunlight-as-food idea and do not treat food and energy as interchangeable.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"Two approved misconceptions both locate food outside the plant, so this block must relocate food-making inside it by separating \\'what the plant takes in\\' from \\'what the plant makes\\' \\\\u2014 a separation a bare definition cannot make.\",\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b2\",\\n      \"intent\": \"explain\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": []\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Build the idea on the same windowsill plant: the roots draw soil materials and water into the plant, but what enters through the roots is raw material the plant uses, not the plant\\'s food. The food itself is made by the plant. Hold the learner\\'s attention on the plant as the maker and treat soil and water only as intakes, so the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings are undermined without being named as errors yet. Do not introduce the sunlight-as-food idea and do not treat food and energy as interchangeable.\",\\n    \"evi