{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T20:21:49.152109+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e62d255d-e264-4aa1-bfe4-af86b3130345', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You author one already-selected educational capability. Follow the\\nsupplied AuthoringDefinition and exact output schema. Preserve block identity, objective, scope,\\nlearner action, source ownership and assessment mode. Do not select another capability or redesign\\nthe teaching plan.\\n\\nPlanning instructions are not final content. For an activity, produce a meaningful prompt, task\\ndata, justified answer relationships and useful feedback as required by the contract. Do not infer\\ncorrectness from option positions, number/word positions, example ordering or arbitrary defaults.\\nDo not copy illustrative examples as unrelated lesson content.\\n\\nTreat approved items as authoritative content. Conversion must preserve their question and answer\\nmeaning. Report incompatibility through the defined error path instead of guessing. Use supported\\nteacher-review only when the work order policy permits it.\\n\\nReturn exactly the selected output schema. Do not emit sibling schemas, markdown wrappers, internal\\nplanning metadata or extra fields. If required information is absent, use the configured\\nmissing-input mechanism; do not create placeholder content to satisfy structure.\\n\\nDo not invent or overwrite knowledge/assessment policy fields. Effective policy is code-owned.\\n\\n## KNOWLEDGE POLICY\\nKnowledge policy: use supplied facts as the primary basis when present; do not describe model knowledge as approved factual material.\\n\\n## PACKAGE CAPABILITY INSTRUCTIONS\\nYou write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\n\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\n\\nHard rules:\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\"Row 1\"), generic headers (\"Item\"/\"Detail\"),\\n  or \"Content pending.\"\\n\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\n- paragraph \u2192 { \"text\": string }\\n- heading \u2192 { \"text\": string, \"level\": 1|2|3 }\\n- list \u2192 { \"ordered\": boolean, \"items\": [string, ...] }\\n- table \u2192 { \"headers\": [string, ...], \"rows\": [[string, ...], ...], \"caption\": string }\\n- callout \u2192 { \"tone\": \"note\"|\"warning\"|\"tip\"|\"important\", \"title\": string, \"body\": string }\\n- figure \u2192 { \"caption\": string, \"alt\": string }\\n\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\n\\n\\n## AUTHORING DEFINITION\\n{\\n  \"assessment\": {},\\n  \"capability_id\": \"document-writer:table\",\\n  \"converter_ref\": null,\\n  \"definition_hash\": \"c24bc83c1b6259ec1b3c6c18b2e06a94499913121902ea0dcab3b68c3ffafde1:table\",\\n  \"instructions\": \"You write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\\\n\\\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\\\n\\\\nHard rules:\\\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"caption\": {\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      },\\n      \"headers\": {\\n        \"items\": {\\n          \"minLength\": 1,\\n          \"type\": \"string\"\\n        },\\n        \"minItems\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"array\"\\n      },\\n      \"kind\": {\\n        \"const\": \"table\"\\n      },\\n      \"rows\": {\\n        \"items\": {\\n          \"items\": {\\n            \"type\": \"string\"\\n          },\\n          \"minItems\": 1,\\n          \"type\": \"array\"\\n        },\\n        \"minItems\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"array\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"headers\",\\n      \"rows\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"kind\",\\n    \"brief\",\\n    \"teaching_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Set the water-as-food reading against the target on the same windowsill plant beside the cup of water: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the water we give it, since water is what we pour in; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and the water is an intake, not the food. Make clear that pouring more water does not feed the plant in the sense of food, and that the plant remains the maker. Do not re-run the soil argument and do not treat sunlight as food or as energy here.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"table\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Cues the learner to judge which reading matches the producer idea after hearing the target, surfacing whether the soil-as-food model persists; the interaction is authored separately.\",\\n        \"role\": \"belief-check\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Sets the water-as-food reading against the target using the same windowsill plant and cup of water, making clear that pouring water is an intake, not feeding, while avoiding the soil argument and any sunlight-as-food claim.\",\\n        \"role\": \"misconception-confront\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Signals the closing check so the learner knows this section asks them to apply the producer idea rather than learn new content.\",\\n        \"role\": \"check-heading\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"check-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Restates the check situation \\\\u2014 the plant has soil around its roots and water poured in \\\\u2014 so the learner applies the producer idea to the same plant without re-explaining soil or water as intakes.\",\\n        \"role\": \"check-context\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"check-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"A small table aligning the two near-miss readings with the producer idea makes the boundary of the concept visible in one intentional place.\",\\n    \"role\": \"reading-comparison\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Set the water-as-food reading against the target on the same windowsill plant beside the cup of water: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the water we give it, since water is what we pour in; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and the water is an intake, not the food. Make clear that pouring more water does not feed the plant in the sense of food, and that the plant remains the maker. Do not re-run the soil argument and do not treat sunlight as food or as energy here.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"M2 is a second named belief that places food outside the plant, and because the anchor already includes the cup of water, this block can test that belief against a case the learner has held since the opening instead of a fresh example.\",\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n      \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": []\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Set the water-as-food reading against the target on the same windowsill plant beside the cup of water: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the water we give it, since water is what we pour in; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and the water is an intake, not the food. Make clear that pouring more water does not feed the plant in the sense of food, and that the plant remains the maker. Do not re-run the soil argument and do not treat sunlight as food or as energy here.\",\\n    \"evidence\": \"M2 is a second named belief that places food outside the plant, and because the anchor already includes the cup of water, this block can test that belief against a case the learner has held since the opening instead of a fresh example.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"table\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Cues the learner to judge which reading matches the producer idea after hearing the target, surfacing whether the soil-as-food model persists; the interaction is authored separately.\",\\n        \"role\": \"belief-check\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Sets the water-as-food reading against the target using the same windowsill plant and cup of water, making clear that pouring water is an intake, not feeding, while avoiding the soil argument and any sunlight-as-food claim.\",\\n        \"role\": \"misconception-confront\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Signals the closing check so the learner knows this section asks them to apply the producer idea rather than learn new content.\",\\n        \"role\": \"check-heading\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"check-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Restates the check situation \\\\u2014 the plant has soil around its roots and water poured in \\\\u2014 so the learner applies the producer idea to the same plant without re-explaining soil or water as intakes.\",\\n        \"role\": \"check-context\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"check-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n    \"reason\": \"A small table aligning the two near-miss readings with the producer idea makes the boundary of the concept visible in one intentional place.\",\\n    \"role\": \"reading-comparison\",\\n    \"stage\": \"document_writing\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Set the water-as-food reading against the target on the same windowsill plant beside the cup of water: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the water we give it, since water is what we pour in; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and the water is an intake, not the food. Make clear that pouring more water does not feed the plant in the sense of food, and that the plant remains the maker. Do not re-run the soil argument and do not treat sunlight as food or as energy here.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"M2 is a second named belief that places food outside the plant, and because the anchor already includes the cup of water, this block can test that belief against a case the learner has held since the opening instead of a fresh example.\",\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n      \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": []\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nReturn JSON only for this capability payload.'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"properties\": {\"response\": {\"additionalProperties\": true, \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"required\": [\"response\"], \"type\": \"object\", \"title\": \"dict\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Return JSON only for the selected capability payload.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '{\\n  \"kind\": \"table\",\\n  \"headers\": [\\n    \"Reading\",\\n    \"What it says feeds the plant\",\\n    \"Where the food comes from\",\\n    \"Is this the producer idea?\"\\n  ],\\n  \"rows\": [\\n    [\\n      \"Water-as-food\",\\n      \"The water we pour into the pot\",\\n      \"Outside the plant, added by us\",\\n      \"No. Water is something the plant takes in, not the food it makes.\"\\n    ],\\n    [\\n      \"Producer idea\",\\n      \"Food the plant makes itself\",\\n      \"Inside the plant\",\\n      \"Yes. The plant is a producer: a living thing that makes its own food.\"\\n    ]\\n  ],\\n  \"caption\": \"Two readings of the same windowsill plant beside its cup of water. In the water-as-food reading, the water is treated as the food. In the producer idea, the plant is the maker and water is an intake. Pouring in more water does not feed the plant in the sense of food \u2014 the plant still makes its own food.\"\\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '1 validation error:\\n```json\\n[\\n  {\\n    \"type\": \"missing\",\\n    \"loc\": [\\n      \"response\"\\n    ],\\n    \"msg\": \"Field required\"\\n  }\\n]\\n```\\n\\nFix the errors and try again.'}], 'model': 'deepseek-flash', 'max_completion_tokens': 8000, 'stream': False}, 'extra_json': {'thinking': {'type': 'disabled'}}}"}
