n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Pose one check that asks the learner to apply the producer idea to the windowsill plant: given that the plant has soil around its roots and water poured in, decide where the plant\\'s food comes from and confirm that the plant is the thing that makes it. Keep the check to this single recognition of the plant as producer, and do not re-explain soil or water as intakes.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner n